 | **Phantom Guardrails**: When Self-Improving Agent Harnesses Fix Failures That Never Happened. [arXiv 2607.13083](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.13083), 13 Jul 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | Carnegie Mellon University, Corespeed Inc., Georgia Tech, independent researchers | A suppression-rewarded proposer invented a guardrail for a rule violation that provably never occurred in 15/60 runs when the input carried a harmless rule-like pattern, against 0/60 on featureless input, across five proposer models. Three conditions must co-occur: a rule-shaped pattern, an open-ended rule set, and an instruction that presupposes failures. Under add-only acceptance the phantom is absorbing; warrant-aware acceptance excludes it. Instrument: the Counterfactual Fabrication Lab, deterministic, with a byte-exact oracle. | The failure mode okl's evidence rule exists to prevent: no stamp without a run, `found_by` on every seed record (a corpus convention the CLI does not yet enforce; see the open work below), and "reproduced" in the evals is a blind cross-model judge's verdict against a stated signal, with the receipt committed. The Claude Code Stop hook's question ("did it surface a lesson worth keeping?") presupposes a lesson, which is their third condition. Their lab is the control condition for any auto-curation experiment. |
 | **DreamBench-SWE**: A Multi-Session Memory-Hygiene Benchmark for Software Agents. [arXiv 2608.20664](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20664), 21 Aug 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | Sarthak Singh, independent researcher | Later tasks depend on non-inferable evidence from earlier sessions, graded by hidden executable oracles. The primary contrast between a typed-plus-raw memory probe and verbatim event memory was null (95/180 against 89/180, p=.518). In the successor audit: no memory 21/180, verbatim event memory 82/180, the reference probe 83/180, a hosted Mem0 literal-storage configuration 97/180. In the original v2 fold, the aggregate executable hygiene diagnostics were StaleUseRate 0.200, RepeatedErrorRate 0.571, OverscopeRate 0.167 and IrrelevantImportRate 0.474. | Staleness is measured here as stale *use* by the agent. DreamBench-SWE does not report whether a memory system *detects* that a stored lesson has gone stale before it briefs; papers outside this reading do (#264). okl's drift is not yet such a detector, only a prompt to re-check: REPORT §4ae measured its precision on this repository's history, and §4af its recall, by breaking lessons on purpose. It caught 14 of 36 breaks, under the 0.5 a re-run must reach before these docs call it detection. The paper's own habit of narrowing its claims is the standard this documentation is written to. |
 | **Memory Transfer Learning**: How Memories are Transferred Across Domains in Coding Agents. [arXiv 2604.14004](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14004), 15 Apr 2026 (pp. 1–3 read) | KAIST, New York University, DeepAuto.ai | A unified memory pool across heterogeneous coding domains improved average scores on 6 benchmarks by 3.7%. The gain comes from meta-knowledge such as validation routines, not task-specific code. Abstraction dictates transferability: Insight-format memories transferred best; raw Trajectories were the weakest format, negative on two of the six benchmarks, which the abstract states as often inducing negative transfer. Transfer scales with pool size and works across models. | A design implication rather than a test of okl: the paper did not evaluate `org` scope, `applies_to` or the Gate type. It argues for recording abstract rules and validation routines, which is what those three exist for, and against recording raw traces as lessons without abstracting them first. |
